cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below"
type: software
title: "CurvaDB: certified curve store and a negative result on splines for trajectory storage"
version: "1.0"
date-released: "2026-09-20"
authors:
  - family-names: "Galukhin"
    given-names: "Artem"
repository-code: "https://github.com/artsensiva/CurvaDB-en"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22850469"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  A cubic B-spline does not compress real GPS trajectories better than a
  Douglas-Peucker-simplified polyline (negative). Removing every confound --
  polyline tethering, fitter weaknesses, noise, sampling sparsity -- still
  does not save the spline; even an oracle fit to the true curve is not more
  compact than DP+SED (negative). A free-knot spline oracle fit directly to
  the true curve still does not beat DP+SED by the required margin, closing
  hypothesis H1 (negative). A certified curve store answers continuous-Frechet
  range queries over compressed trajectories with a provable error bound,
  correctly and cheaper than an honest uncompressed exact competitor
  (positive). That same store's spec-designated primary spline certificate
  mechanism does not work as designed, and the correctness guarantee has a
  real, measured price (negative). Full detail: docs/REPORT.md.
keywords:
  - trajectory data
  - Frechet distance
  - B-splines
  - certified bounds
  - negative results
  - GeoLife
